  "commentary": "Recorded in the summer of 1969, *Abbey Road* is the eleventh studio album by The Beatles and the final album they recorded together as a group (though *Let It Be* was released later in 1970). It represents the peak of their studio craftsmanship under producer George Martin, recorded on a new solid-state TG12345 mixing console which gave the album a warmer, smoother, and more modern low-end sound than their previous 8-track recordings.\n\nSide 1 features legendary individual songwriting achievements, including John Lennon's heavy blues-rock \"Come Together,\" George Harrison's breakthrough masterpiece \"Something,\" and Paul McCartney's intense vocal showcase \"Oh! Darling.\" Side 2 is defined by the famous 16-minute medley—a series of short, unfinished songs woven together by McCartney and producer George Martin into a cohesive suite, culminating in \"The End\" and the unlisted snippet \"Her Majesty.\"\n\nThis 2019 50th Anniversary release features a new stereo mix by Giles Martin (George Martin's son) and Sam Okell, sourced directly from the original eight-track session tapes. The remastering and lacquer cutting were performed by Miles Showell at Abbey Road Studios using half-speed mastering, which provides improved high-frequency response and stereo definition.\n\n**About this 2021 US pressing (1×LP, Reissue, Repress, Remastered, Stereo, 180g):** Apple Records/Capitol Records/UMe catalog `B0030719-01` (with Side A label carrying `B0030719-01A`). This is a repress manufactured at **GZ Media** in the Czech Republic, using the same half-speed master metalwork cut by Miles Showell at Abbey Road Studios. It is identified by the GZ Media job stamps (`214816E` / `214818E`), the European set number `7791512` etched in the runout, and GZ-specific pressing/job codes (like `2211919` on Side A and `02218420` on Side B).\n\nPressed on 180g vinyl and housed in a faithful reproduction of the original gatefold sleeve, the album features the iconic cover photo of the band walking across the zebra crossing outside Abbey Road Studios. It includes a printed inner sleeve with lyrics and credits.\n",
